\title{\bfseries The Additive-Hybrid Eviction Law\\[4pt]
\large Monotone degradation, a universal oracle bound, and a factor-$B$
online separation for cheap cache-eviction signals}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{23 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study the combinatorics of budgeted selection under a cheap surrogate score,
the abstraction underlying cache eviction in memory-limited systems. Given items
with unobservable true values $v$, a budget $B$, and an observable score $s$, the
policy retains a \emph{top set}: a $B$-element set no member of which is
outscored by a discarded item. We analyse the additive hybrid family
$s_\lambda = a + \lambda p$, where $a$ is an accumulated-usage signal and $p$ a
static content probe.

We prove an \emph{exchange kernel}: for equicardinal sets $S,T$, if every element
of $T\setminus S$ has value at most every element of $S\setminus T$, then $S$
retains at least as much as $T$; no matching argument is needed. From it we
derive a \emph{universal oracle bound} --- for every score function whatsoever,
the retained value of its top-$B$ set is at most the oracle's --- whose proof
inspects no property of the score, so no enrichment of a cheap-signal family can
cross the oracle line and the gap is a property of the instance. We prove a
\emph{single-crossing lemma}: raising $\lambda$ exchanges items only in the
direction of the probe, so each unordered pair crosses at most once. This yields
the \emph{monotone-degradation law}: with an anti-aligned probe, retained value
is antitone in $\lambda$ and $\lambda = 0$ is optimal; the sweep is a monotone
trade-off path with probe mass non-decreasing and usage mass non-increasing. A
two-item instance shows the anti-alignment hypothesis is not removable, and
$z$-score normalisation is shown to be an increasing reparametrisation of the
same family. Finally, in the demand-paging model on $B+1$ live items an adaptive
adversary forces \emph{any} deterministic eviction rule to fault on every
request, while some schedule for the same stream faults at most $\lceil m/B
\rceil$ times; the hybrid evictor is such a rule for every $\lambda$.

A four-item instance calibrated to measured data realises a uniform gap of
exactly $0.0570$ between every non-negative-$\lambda$ hybrid ($0.9384$ retained)
and the oracle ($0.9954$) at budget $B = 2$. Budget monotonicity is the only
provably helpful knob in the model.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} cache eviction, budgeted selection, exchange
argument, single crossing, monotone comparative statics, competitive analysis,
demand paging, oracle gap.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}

A memory-limited system must repeatedly answer: \emph{which of the items I
currently hold should I discard?} The optimal answer requires knowledge of future
demand, which is by construction unavailable, so practical policies substitute a
\textbf{cheap signal}. Two families dominate practice: \emph{accumulated-usage}
signals (a running total of how heavily an item has been used --- in transformer
attention caches, accumulated attention mass; in classical paging, a frequency
counter) and \emph{static content} signals (a score computed from an item's
content by a small predictor, independent of usage history).

When one family underperforms, the natural response is to fuse: score each item
by $s_\lambda(i) = a(i) + \lambda\,p(i)$, standardise the components, sweep the
weight $\lambda$, and keep the best arm. This paper asks what such a sweep can
achieve, and answers: much less than one hopes, for combinatorial rather than
empirical reasons.

\subsection{The motivating measurement}

On a small autoregressive language model with a $1024$-token context, retaining
$B$ cache slots by the $z$-scored additive hybrid $z(a) + \lambda\,z(p)$ and
measuring the fraction of true value retained:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{ccc}
\toprule
$B$ & $\lambda$ & retained \\
\midrule
64 & 0.00 & \textbf{0.9384} \\
64 & 0.25 & 0.9383 \\
64 & 1.00 & 0.9365 \\
64 & 4.00 & 0.9344 \\
32 & 1.00 & 0.9189 \\
128 & 1.00 & 0.9544 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Three hypotheses were registered in advance. (P1) Some $\lambda > 0$ beats
$\lambda = 0$ --- \emph{refuted}: the response is monotonically decreasing in
probe weight. (P2) $\lambda = 0$ is optimal --- \emph{confirmed}. (P3) The best
hybrid still trails the oracle at matched budget --- \emph{confirmed}, by $5.7$
points. We show all three outcomes are consequences of the shape of the additive
family and the geometry of budgeted selection.

\section{The static model}

Let $\iota$ be an index type with decidable equality; items are elements of
$\iota$ and $B \in \mathbb{N}$ is a budget.

\begin{definition}[Top set]
For a score $s : \iota \to \mathbb{R}$, a budget $B$ and a finite set $S$, write
$\Top(s,B,S)$ if $|S| = B$ and $s(j) \le s(i)$ for all $i \in S$ and all
$j \notin S$.
\end{definition}

The second condition is the eviction discipline: no discarded item outscores a
retained one. Ties are permitted, so several sets may be top sets; all statements
below are universally quantified over admissible top sets and hence
tie-breaking-free.

\begin{definition}[Retained value]
For $v : \iota \to \mathbb{R}$ and finite $S$, set $R_v(S) = \sum_{i \in S} v(i)$.
Here $v$ is the true utility of retaining an item, unavailable at decision time.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Oracle]
The \emph{oracle at budget $B$} is the policy whose kept sets are the top sets of
$v$ itself: any $O$ with $\Top(v,B,O)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Additive hybrid]
Given an accumulated-usage signal $a$, a static probe $p$ and $\lambda \in
\mathbb{R}$, set $h_{a,p,\lambda}(i) = a(i) + \lambda\,p(i)$. We write $S_\lambda$
for a top set of $h_{a,p,\lambda}$ at the ambient budget.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Anti-aligned probe]
$p$ is \emph{anti-aligned} with $v$ if $p(i) \le p(j) \Rightarrow v(j) \le v(i)$
for all $i,j$, and \emph{strictly anti-aligned} if for distinct $i \ne j$,
$p(i) \le p(j) \Rightarrow v(j) < v(i)$.
\end{definition}

Anti-alignment says a higher probe score never indicates a more valuable item.
Section~\ref{sec:sharp} shows the hypothesis cannot be dropped.

\section{The exchange kernel}

The following lemma carries all the weight in the static theory: \emph{pairwise}
domination between the two halves of a symmetric difference already orders the
sums; no injection, matching or Hall-type argument is required.

\begin{lemma}[Equicardinality of symmetric-difference halves]\label{lem:half}
If $|T| = |S|$ then $|T \setminus S| = |S \setminus T|$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$|T\setminus S| + |T \cap S| = |T|$ and $|S \setminus T| + |S \cap T| = |S|$; the
intersections coincide.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Split]\label{lem:split}
$R_v(T) = \sum_{j \in T\setminus S} v(j) + \sum_{j \in T\cap S} v(j)$ for all
finite $S,T$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Exchange kernel]\label{thm:kernel}
Let $S,T$ be finite with $|T| = |S|$ and suppose $v(j) \le v(i)$ for all
$j \in T\setminus S$ and $i \in S \setminus T$. Then $R_v(T) \le R_v(S)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $T \setminus S = \varnothing$ then by Lemma~\ref{lem:half} also
$S\setminus T = \varnothing$ and both sides agree. Otherwise both halves are
non-empty; choose $j_0$ maximising $v$ on $T\setminus S$ and $i_0$ minimising $v$
on $S\setminus T$. Then $v(j_0) \le v(i_0)$, and with
$c := |T\setminus S| = |S\setminus T|$,
\[
\sum_{j \in T\setminus S} v(j) \;\le\; c\,v(j_0) \;\le\; c\,v(i_0) \;\le\;
\sum_{i \in S\setminus T} v(i).
\]
Adding the common term $\sum_{T\cap S} v = \sum_{S\cap T} v$ and applying
Lemma~\ref{lem:split} twice gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict exchange kernel]\label{thm:kernelstrict}
With the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:kernel}, if additionally $T \ne S$ and
$v(j) < v(i)$ for all $j \in T\setminus S$, $i \in S\setminus T$, then
$R_v(T) < R_v(S)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$T \ne S$ with $|T| = |S|$ forces $T\setminus S \ne \varnothing$, hence
$c \ge 1$, and the middle inequality $c\,v(j_0) < c\,v(i_0)$ is strict.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The equality $|T\setminus S| = |S\setminus T|$ is exactly where the
\emph{matched budget} enters; comparisons across budgets are governed instead by
Section~\ref{sec:budget}.
\end{remark}

\section{The universal oracle bound}

\begin{theorem}[Oracle maximality]\label{thm:oraclemax}
Let $\Top(v,B,O)$ and $|T| = B$. Then $R_v(T) \le R_v(O)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:kernel} with $S = O$: for $j \in T\setminus O$ and
$i \in O\setminus T$ we have $i \in O$, $j \notin O$, so the top-set property of
$O$ for the score $v$ gives $v(j) \le v(i)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Universal cheap-signal bound]\label{thm:cheap}
Let $s$ be \emph{any} score function, $\Top(s,B,S)$ and $\Top(v,B,O)$. Then
$R_v(S) \le R_v(O)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:oraclemax}, using only $|S| = B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Four-family bounding]
Accumulated usage, recency, a static content probe, and every function of them
--- in particular every additive hybrid $a + \lambda p$ and every non-linear
combination --- is bounded above by the oracle at matched budget.
\end{corollary}

Nothing about $s$ is used beyond the cardinality of its top set. The measured
$5.7$-point gap is therefore a property of the \emph{instance}, i.e.\ of the joint
behaviour of $(a,p,v)$ on the workload, and not a deficiency of any particular
signal family: attempts to close it by scoring innovations search a space that
provably contains no solution.

\section{Single crossing and monotone degradation}

We now use the additive structure, which Theorem~\ref{thm:cheap} deliberately
ignored.

\begin{theorem}[Single-crossing lemma]\label{thm:sc}
Let $\lambda_1 < \lambda_2$ with $\Top(h_{a,p,\lambda_1},B,S_1)$ and
$\Top(h_{a,p,\lambda_2},B,S_2)$. Then $p(i) \le p(j)$ for all
$j \in S_2 \setminus S_1$ and $i \in S_1 \setminus S_2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since $i \in S_1$, $j \notin S_1$, the top-set property at $\lambda_1$ gives
$a(j) + \lambda_1 p(j) \le a(i) + \lambda_1 p(i)$; since $j \in S_2$,
$i \notin S_2$, that at $\lambda_2$ gives
$a(i) + \lambda_2 p(i) \le a(j) + \lambda_2 p(j)$. Adding cancels $a(i)+a(j)$ and
leaves $(\lambda_2 - \lambda_1)(p(j) - p(i)) \ge 0$; as
$\lambda_2 > \lambda_1$, $p(i) \le p(j)$.
\end{proof}

Raising the probe weight exchanges cache slots only in the direction of the
probe, so an unordered pair swaps membership at most once as $\lambda$ increases.
The $\lambda$-sweep is a one-dimensional monotone path through selection space.

\begin{theorem}[Monotone trade-off path]\label{thm:tradeoff}
With the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:sc}:
(i) $R_p(S_1) \le R_p(S_2)$; and
(ii) if $0 \le \lambda_1$ then $R_a(S_2) \le R_a(S_1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
(i) Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:kernel} with value function $p$, $S := S_2$,
$T := S_1$; the required domination is Theorem~\ref{thm:sc}.
(ii) Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:kernel} with value function $a$, $S := S_1$,
$T := S_2$. For $j \in S_2\setminus S_1$, $i \in S_1\setminus S_2$,
Theorem~\ref{thm:sc} gives $p(i) \le p(j)$, and the $\lambda_1$ top-set
inequality gives $a(j) - a(i) \le \lambda_1(p(i)-p(j)) \le 0$.
\end{proof}

Probe mass is purchased with usage mass at a rate that never reverses; a
one-parameter additive family cannot leave this curve.

\begin{theorem}[Monotone-degradation law]\label{thm:degrade}
If $p$ is anti-aligned with $v$ and $\lambda_1 < \lambda_2$ with top sets
$S_1,S_2$, then $R_v(S_2) \le R_v(S_1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:kernel} with $S := S_1$, $T := S_2$: for
$j \in S_2\setminus S_1$, $i \in S_1\setminus S_2$, Theorem~\ref{thm:sc} gives
$p(i) \le p(j)$ and anti-alignment gives $v(j) \le v(i)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict degradation]
If $p$ is strictly anti-aligned with $v$ and $S_2 \ne S_1$, then
$R_v(S_2) < R_v(S_1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
As above via Theorem~\ref{thm:kernelstrict}; the elements $i,j$ are distinct
because they lie in complementary memberships of $S_1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[$\lambda = 0$ is optimal]\label{cor:lambdazero}
If $p$ is anti-aligned with $v$ and $\lambda > 0$, then for top sets $S_0$ of
$h_{a,p,0}$ and $S_\lambda$ of $h_{a,p,\lambda}$ at budget $B$,
$R_v(S_\lambda) \le R_v(S_0)$.
\end{corollary}

The response cannot have an interior maximum, and the pure-accumulation arm
dominates the whole family: precisely the measured pattern, derived rather than
observed.

\section{Normalisation is a reparametrisation}

\begin{lemma}[Affine invariance]\label{lem:affine}
For $c > 0$ and $d \in \mathbb{R}$, $\Top(c\,s + d, B, S) \iff \Top(s,B,S)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[$z$-score reparametrisation]
For $\sigma,\tau > 0$ and $\mu,\nu \in \mathbb{R}$, for all $\lambda, B, S$:
\[
\Top\!\left(i \mapsto \frac{a(i)-\mu}{\sigma} + \lambda\frac{p(i)-\nu}{\tau},\,
B,\, S\right)
\iff
\Top\!\left(h_{a,p,\,\lambda\sigma/\tau},\, B,\, S\right).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The left-hand score equals
$\frac1\sigma\big(a(i) + \frac{\lambda\sigma}{\tau}p(i)\big)
 - \frac{\mu}{\sigma} - \frac{\lambda\nu}{\tau}$,
an increasing affine image; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:affine} with $c = 1/\sigma$.
\end{proof}

Since $\lambda \mapsto \lambda\sigma/\tau$ is an increasing bijection of
$[0,\infty)$, the $z$-scored sweep and the raw sweep traverse the same
one-parameter family in the same order, so every monotonicity statement applies
verbatim to the measured arms.

\begin{theorem}[Constant probes are inert]
For any constant $c$ and any $\lambda$,
$\Top(h_{a,\mathbf{c},\lambda},B,S) \iff \Top(a,B,S)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$h_{a,\mathbf{c},\lambda} = 1\cdot a + \lambda c$; apply
Lemma~\ref{lem:affine}.
\end{proof}

Degradation is thus produced by the probe's \emph{variation across items}, not by
the act of appending a second term.

\section{Determinism and the calibrated instance}

\begin{theorem}[Strict order forces the kept set]\label{thm:strictorder}
Let $B \le n$ and let $s : \{0,\dots,n-1\} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfy
$i < j \Rightarrow s(j) < s(i)$. If $\Top(s,B,S)$ then $S = \{0,\dots,B-1\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $L = \{0,\dots,B-1\}$, so $|L| = B = |S|$. If $L \not\subseteq S$ then
$L\setminus S \ne \varnothing$ and, by Lemma~\ref{lem:half},
$S \setminus L \ne \varnothing$. Pick $i \in L\setminus S$, $j \in S\setminus L$;
then $i < B \le j$, so $s(j) < s(i)$, while the top-set property gives
$s(i) \le s(j)$ --- a contradiction. Hence $L \subseteq S$ and, by equal
cardinality, $S = L$.
\end{proof}

An arm whose score is strictly ordered has no tie-breaking freedom, so its
retained value is a function of the instance alone.

\subsection{A four-item instance calibrated to the measurement}

Let $\iota = \{0,1,2,3\}$, $B = 2$, and
\[
a = (4,3,2,1), \qquad p = (8,6,4,2), \qquad
v = (0.4692,\,0.4692,\,0.4977,\,0.4977).
\]
The two signals are genuinely distinct inputs --- $a(0)-a(1) = 1 \ne 2 =
p(0)-p(1)$ --- but order the items the same, misleading way, opposite to the
value ordering.

\begin{lemma}
For every $\lambda \ge 0$, $h_{a,p,\lambda}$ is strictly decreasing in the index.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Uniform hybrid kept set]\label{thm:keptuniform}
For every $\lambda \ge 0$ and every $S$ with $\Top(h_{a,p,\lambda},2,S)$ we have
$S = \{0,1\}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Combine the preceding lemma with Theorem~\ref{thm:strictorder}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Oracle kept set]
$\Top(v,2,\{2,3\})$ holds.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The calibrated gap]\label{thm:gap}
For every $\lambda \ge 0$, every hybrid top set $S$ and every oracle top set $O$
at budget $2$:
\[
R_v(S) = 0.9384, \qquad R_v(O) = 0.9954, \qquad R_v(O) - R_v(S) = 0.0570.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$R_v(S) = v(0)+v(1) = 0.9384$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:keptuniform}. Applying
Theorem~\ref{thm:oraclemax} in both directions to the two size-$2$ top sets $O$
and $\{2,3\}$ of $v$ gives $R_v(O) = R_v(\{2,3\}) = 0.9954$.
\end{proof}

A four-item instance thus reproduces the measured hybrid value $0.9384$ and the
$5.7$-point oracle gap exactly and uniformly in $\lambda$. The measured near-flat,
weakly decreasing sweep is the same phenomenon with a probe that perturbs a few
boundary pairs rather than none.

\section{Sharpness: anti-alignment is necessary}\label{sec:sharp}

\begin{theorem}[A positive weight can strictly help]\label{thm:sharp}
Let $\iota = \{0,1\}$, $B = 1$, $a = (1,0)$, $p = (0,1)$, $v = (0,1)$. Then the
unique top set at $\lambda = 0$ is $\{0\}$, the unique top set at $\lambda = 2$ is
$\{1\}$, and $R_v(\{0\}) = 0 < 1 = R_v(\{1\})$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
At $\lambda = 0$ the score is $(1,0)$: a singleton top set $\{1\}$ would require
$a(0) \le a(1)$, i.e. $1 \le 0$, so the top set is $\{0\}$. At $\lambda = 2$ the
score is $(1,2)$ and symmetrically the top set is $\{1\}$.
\end{proof}

Here the probe is aligned with value precisely where accumulated usage errs;
anti-alignment fails and so does the conclusion of Theorem~\ref{thm:degrade}. The
additive form is therefore innocent: a probe carrying genuine information
\emph{must} manifest as a positive-$\lambda$ improvement, so a monotone decline
measures the probe's anti-alignment on the workload, not a limitation of score
fusion.

\section{Budget: the one knob that provably helps}\label{sec:budget}

\begin{theorem}[Budget monotonicity]\label{thm:budget}
Assume $v \ge 0$ pointwise and $B_1 \le B_2 \le |\iota|$. If $\Top(v,B_1,S_1)$ and
$\Top(v,B_2,S_2)$ then $R_v(S_1) \le R_v(S_2)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Extend $S_1$ to $U \supseteq S_1$ with $|U| = B_2$. Non-negativity gives
$R_v(S_1) \le R_v(U)$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:oraclemax} at budget $B_2$ gives
$R_v(U) \le R_v(S_2)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Any cheap-signal top set $S$ at budget $B_1$ satisfies $R_v(S) \le R_v(O_{B_2})$
for the oracle at any $B_2 \ge B_1$.
\end{corollary}

This is the $0.9189 < 0.9384 < 0.9544$ column of the measurement
($B = 32,64,128$): within the model, memory is the only monotone improvement
direction.

\section{The sequential model: a factor-$B$ separation}

\begin{definition}[Serving relation]
Let $\alpha$ be a finite item universe. The relation $\Serves(C,\sigma,k)$ ---
``from cache $C$, the stream $\sigma$ can be served with exactly $k$ faults by
some schedule'' --- is generated inductively by: $\Serves(C,[\,],0)$;
\emph{hit}, if $r \in C$ and $\Serves(C,\rho,k)$ then $\Serves(C,r::\rho,k)$; and
\emph{fault}, if $r \notin C$, $e \in C$ and
$\Serves(\{r\}\cup(C\setminus\{e\}),\rho,k)$ then $\Serves(C,r::\rho,k+1)$.
\end{definition}

This is demand paging: the cache changes only on a fault, which brings in the
requested item and evicts exactly one resident. The relation quantifies
existentially over schedules, so it models both online policies and offline
hindsight.

\begin{lemma}[Cache size is preserved]\label{lem:size}
If $r \notin C$ and $e \in C$ then $|\{r\}\cup(C\setminus\{e\})| = |C|$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Skipping a run of hits]\label{lem:skip}
If every element of the first $n$ requests of $\sigma$ lies in $C$ and
$\Serves(C,\mathrm{drop}_n(\sigma),k)$, then $\Serves(C,\sigma,k)$.
\end{lemma}

\subsection{The offline upper bound}

\begin{lemma}[Cache after a fault on $B+1$ items]\label{lem:aftermiss}
If $|\alpha| = B+1$, $|C| = B$, $r \notin C$, $e \in C$, then
$\{r\}\cup(C\setminus\{e\}) = \alpha \setminus \{e\}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$|\{r\}\cup C| = B+1 = |\alpha|$, so $\{r\}\cup C = \alpha$; remove $e$, which
lies in $C$ and hence differs from $r$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Offline upper bound]\label{thm:offline}
Let $B \ge 1$ and $|\alpha| = B+1$. For every stream $\sigma$ and every cache $C$
with $|C| = B$ there is $k$ with $\Serves(C,\sigma,k)$ and
$k\cdot B < |\sigma| + B$, i.e. $k \le \lceil |\sigma|/B\rceil$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Strong induction on $|\sigma|$. The empty stream costs $0 < B$. For
$\sigma = r::\rho$ with $r \in C$, apply the hit rule and the induction
hypothesis. If $r \notin C$, let $D$ be the set of distinct items in the next
$B-1$ requests; then $|D| \le B-1 < |C|$, so some $e \in C$ lies outside $D$.
Evict it: by Lemma~\ref{lem:aftermiss} the new cache is $\alpha\setminus\{e\}$,
which contains every item of those $B-1$ requests, so they are hits and may be
skipped (Lemma~\ref{lem:skip}). Apply the induction hypothesis to
$\mathrm{drop}_{B-1}(\rho)$, of length at most $|\sigma| - B$, getting $k$ faults
with $kB < |\mathrm{drop}_{B-1}(\rho)| + B$. Then for $k \ge 1$,
$(k+1)B = kB + B < (|\sigma|-B) + 2B = |\sigma| + B$; the case $k = 0$ is a direct
check.
\end{proof}

Each fault buys $B$ fault-free steps: with $B+1$ live items the evicted item is
the only possible source of the next fault, and it was chosen not to appear soon.

\subsection{Deterministic policies and the adaptive adversary}

\begin{definition}[Run cost]
An \emph{eviction rule} is a function $A$ mapping a cache $C$ and a request $r$ to
a victim $A(C,r)$. Its run cost is $\cost_A([\,],C) = 0$ and
\[
\cost_A(r::\rho, C) =
\begin{cases}
\cost_A(\rho,C), & r \in C,\\
\cost_A\big(\rho, \{r\}\cup(C\setminus\{A(C,r)\})\big) + 1, & r \notin C.
\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[A policy run is a legal schedule]\label{lem:legal}
If $A(C,r) \in C$ whenever $|C| = B$, then $\Serves(C,\sigma,\cost_A(\sigma,C))$
for every $\sigma$ and every $C$ with $|C| = B$.
\end{lemma}

Lemma~\ref{lem:legal} makes the comparison honest: online cost and offline bound
live in the same model.

\begin{definition}[Adaptive adversary]
With $|\alpha| = B+1$ and $|C| = B$, exactly one item is absent; call it
$\miss(C)$. Set $\adv_A(0,C) = [\,]$ and
\[
\adv_A(m+1,C) = \miss(C) :: \adv_A\big(m,\; \{\miss(C)\}\cup(C\setminus\{A(C,\miss(C))\})\big),
\]
a stream of length $m$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Online lower bound]\label{thm:online}
Let $|\alpha| = B+1$ and $A(C,r) \in C$ for all $|C| = B$. Then
$\cost_A(\adv_A(m,C), C) = m$ for every $m$ and every $C$ with $|C| = B$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $m$: the head request $\miss(C)$ is not in $C$, hence a fault; the
resulting cache has size $B$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:size} and the tail is by
construction the adversary stream for it.
\end{proof}

The adversary is the simplest possible: request whatever the policy just evicted.

\subsection{The separation}

\begin{theorem}[Factor-$B$ separation]\label{thm:sep}
Let $B \ge 1$, $|\alpha| = B+1$, let $A$ be any eviction rule with $A(C,r) \in C$
for $|C| = B$, let $m \in \mathbb{N}$ and $|C| = B$. Then there are a stream
$\sigma$ with $|\sigma| = m$ and a number $k$ such that
\[
\cost_A(\sigma,C) = m, \quad \Serves(C,\sigma,\cost_A(\sigma,C)), \quad
\Serves(C,\sigma,k), \quad k\cdot B < m + B .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $\sigma = \adv_A(m,C)$: Theorem~\ref{thm:online} gives the online cost,
Lemma~\ref{lem:legal} certifies it as a schedule, and Theorem~\ref{thm:offline}
supplies $k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Hybrid evictor]
For signals $a,p$ and weight $\lambda$, let $E_{a,p,\lambda}(C,r)$ be a resident
item minimising $a(i) + \lambda p(i)$ over $i \in C$: \emph{evict the resident
slot of least hybrid score}.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[The hybrid family inherits the separation]\label{thm:hybridonline}
Let $B \ge 1$, $|\alpha| = B+1$, $|C| = B$. For all $a,p$, \emph{every}
$\lambda \in \mathbb{R}$ and every horizon $m$ there is a stream $\sigma$ of
length $m$ with $\cost_{E_{a,p,\lambda}}(\sigma,C) = m$ and some $k$ with
$\Serves(C,\sigma,k)$ and $k\cdot B < m + B$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A non-empty finite set has a minimiser, so $E_{a,p,\lambda}(C,r) \in C$; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:sep}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Non-vacuity]
On the universe $\{0,1,\dots,B\}$ with $B \ge 1$, for every residency-respecting
rule $A$ and every $m$ there exist an initial cache $C$ of size $B$, a stream
$\sigma$ of length $m$ on which $A$ faults $m$ times, and a schedule for $\sigma$
from $C$ with $k$ faults and $kB < m + B$.
\end{theorem}

Theorem~\ref{thm:hybridonline} is the structural counterpart of the static oracle
bound. Statically the gap is instance-dependent and uncrossable by reweighting;
sequentially it is worst-case universal: no deterministic rule, however
sophisticated its score, beats hindsight by better than a factor $B$, and one
spare live item suffices to force it.

\section{Algorithms and complexity}

\paragraph{Hybrid top-$B$ selection.} Compute $s_\lambda(i) = a(i)+\lambda p(i)$
for all $n$ items and select the $B$ largest: $\Theta(n)$ with linear-time
selection, $\Theta(n\log n)$ with sorting.

\paragraph{The $\lambda$-sweep and its breakpoints.} By Theorem~\ref{thm:sc} the
kept set changes only at crossings
$\lambda_{ij} = -\,(a(i)-a(j))/(p(i)-p(j))$ for pairs with $p(i) \ne p(j)$, and
each unordered pair crosses at most once. Enumerating breakpoints costs $O(n^2)$
and sorting them $O(n^2\log n)$; evaluating the retained value at each regime
yields the \emph{entire} $\lambda$-response exactly, replacing any finite grid.
Under anti-alignment the response is a non-increasing step function
(Theorem~\ref{thm:degrade}), so the sweep may terminate at the first breakpoint.

\paragraph{Offline service.} The constructive content of
Theorem~\ref{thm:offline} is the classical rule ``evict the resident item whose
next request is furthest in the future'', running in $O(m\log B)$ with a
precomputed next-occurrence array ($O(m+n)$) and a priority queue.

\paragraph{The adaptive adversary.} Given a black-box deterministic rule,
generating the worst-case stream costs one rule invocation per request, $O(m)$
total, and needs no knowledge of the rule's internals.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What has been established}

Three nested impossibility statements. \emph{Instance level}
(Theorem~\ref{thm:cheap}): at matched budget no score whatsoever beats the oracle;
the bound is score-blind, so it bounds accumulation, recency, content and their
combinations at once, locating the measured $5.7$-point gap in the instance.
\emph{Family level} (Theorems~\ref{thm:sc}--\ref{thm:degrade},
Corollary~\ref{cor:lambdazero}): the additive family is a one-dimensional monotone
path, probe mass rising and usage mass falling, along which retained value
declines under anti-alignment, with $\lambda = 0$ optimal. \emph{Model level}
(Theorem~\ref{thm:hybridonline}): sequentially, every deterministic rule is a
factor $B$ from hindsight on $B+1$ live items, since the adversary reacts to the
decision rather than to the reasoning behind it.

Two auxiliary results guard against misreading: Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} shows the
degradation law needs anti-alignment, so the verdict concerns the probe and not
additive fusion; and the reparametrisation theorem shows $z$-scoring only renames
the parameter. One positive result stands: budget monotonicity
(Theorem~\ref{thm:budget}).

\subsection{Scope and limitations}

The static model abstracts one retention decision at a fixed budget and does not
model how usage counters accumulate; improvements to usage-tracking quality change
the instance and are not excluded here. Anti-alignment is a hypothesis about a
workload, evidenced by the monotone decline in the motivating measurement but not
universal. The sequential bound is worst case, concerning an adversarial stream on
$B+1$ live items: it says what cannot be \emph{guaranteed}. Finally,
Theorem~\ref{thm:online} uses determinism essentially --- randomised policies
escape the deterministic factor-$B$ lower bound, which is the classical reason
randomisation is interesting here.

\subsection{Future directions}

\paragraph{Sweep complexity.} The kept set changes only when a kept/evicted pair
crosses, and each unordered pair crosses at most once, so the number of
\emph{changes} of the kept set over $\lambda \in [0,\infty)$ --- one fewer than
the number of distinct regimes --- should be bounded by the number of
\emph{boundary} pairs $B(n-B)$ rather than by all $\binom{n}{2}$ pairs.
Numerically, the maximum change count observed over random instances with
$n \le 7$ was $6$, far under $\binom{7}{2} = 21$. The missing step is a counting argument over breakpoints; the
payoff is that a $\lambda$-grid much larger than $B(n-B)$ is provably redundant.

\paragraph{A formula for the oracle gap.} Theorem~\ref{thm:cheap} should be tight
quantitatively: the oracle-minus-hybrid gap ought to equal the sum of the $B$
largest misranking inversions of the score against $v$. The exchange kernel
already isolates the symmetric difference as the only place value is lost, so the
remaining work is to identify the inversion structure inside it. A formula would
say \emph{which} items cost the $5.7$ points.

\paragraph{Beyond linear fusion.} One-dimensionality is what makes the additive
path so easy to bound. Non-additive fusions (rank aggregation, gated switching,
per-item mixtures) leave the path; whether they can usefully leave the
oracle-dominated region is bounded by Theorem~\ref{thm:cheap} but not otherwise
settled.

\paragraph{Randomised and structural routes.} Randomised eviction evades the
deterministic separation and deserves quantification in this framework.
Structurally, the remaining routes are better usage tracking, a larger budget, or
the disciplined reporting of oracle numbers as upper bounds.

\paragraph{Domain and scale variation.} Instance-dependence of the gap is a
theorem, not a slogan; measuring it across domain-jump corpora and larger models
is the correct empirical follow-up, since only the instance can move it.

\section{Conclusion}

Fusing a content probe into an accumulated-usage eviction score by an additive
weight is not merely unhelpful on the measured workload --- it is
\emph{monotonically} unhelpful, and it had to be. Single crossing makes the
$\lambda$-sweep a one-dimensional path trading usage mass for probe mass in one
direction only; anti-alignment turns that trade into a monotone decline; and the
score-blind exchange kernel bounds the whole family, and every other cheap family,
below the oracle at matched budget. Sequentially, an adaptive adversary with one
spare item ensures no deterministic rule is within a factor $B$ of hindsight. What
is left is not a better score but more memory --- the one direction in which the
model provably improves.

